===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To come or go back (to a place or person).
#: {{ux|en|Although the birds fly north for the summer, they '''return''' here in winter.}}
#* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=IV|page=58|passage=The Celebrity, by arts unknown, induced Mrs. Judge Short and two other ladies to call at Mohair on a certain afternoon when Mr. Cooke was trying a trotter on the track. The three '''returned''' wondering and charmed with Mrs. Cooke; they were sure she had had no hand in the furnishing of that atrocious house.}}
#* {{quote-web|url=https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/48749454|en|year=1942|work={{w|The Advertiser (Adelaide)|The Advertiser}}|title=‘I Came Through; I Shall Return|passage=“I came through and I shall '''return''',” General {{w|Douglas MacArthur|MacArthur}} declared when he spoke at Terowie of the beleagured Philippines.}}
#* {{RQ:Allingham China Governess|chapter=Meeting Point|page=232|passage=As soon as Julia '''returned''' with a constable, Timothy, who was on the point of exhaustion, prepared to give over to him gratefully. The newcomer turned out to be a powerful youngster, fully trained and eager to help, and he stripped off his tunic at once.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To go back in [[thought]], [[narration]], or [[argument]].
#: {{ux|en|To '''return''' to my story{{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[recur]]; to come again.
#: {{ux|en|Winter '''returns''' every year.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|obsolete}} To [[turn back]], [[retreat]].
#* {{RQ:Malory Le Morte Darthur|book=V|passage=‘I suppose here is none woll be glad to '''returne''' – and as for me,’ seyde Sir Cador, ‘I had lever dye this day that onys to turne my bak.’}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To turn (something) round.
#* {{RQ:Malory Le Morte Darthur|book=X| chapter=xiij|passage=Whan Kyng Marke harde hym sey that worde, he '''returned''' his horse and abode by hym.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[place]] or [[put back]] something where it had been.
#: {{ux|en|Please '''return''' your hands to your lap.}}
#: {{ux|en|Strain the stock and '''return''' it to the pan.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To give something [[back]] to its original holder or owner.
#: {{ux|en|You should '''return''' the library book within one month.}}
# To give in [[requital]] or [[recompense]]; to [[requite]].
#* {{RQ:KJV|1 Kings|2|44|page=441|column=2|text={{...}}the {{smc|Lord}} ſhall '''returne''' thy wickedneſſe vpon thine owne head.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[reciprocate]] (a [[visit]] or [[telephone call]], electronic message, etc.).
#: {{ux|en|Hello, I’m just '''returning''' your call. What did you want to talk about?}}
#: {{ux|en|I wrote to my ex-wife but she won't '''return''' my emails.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[take back]] something to a [[vendor]] for a [[complete]] or [[partial]] [[refund]].
#: {{ux|en|Yeah, it’s $600,000 but, if it doesn’t work, you can always '''return''' it. As long as it’s undamaged and in the original packaging, I’ll give you a full refund.}}
# {{lb|en|tennis}} To bat the ball back over the [[net]] in response to a [[serve]].
#: {{ux|en|The player couldn’t '''return''' the serve because it was so fast.}}
# {{lb|en|card games}} To play a [[card]] as a result of another player’s [[lead]].
#: {{ux|en|If one players plays a trump, the others must '''return''' a trump.}}
# {{lb|en|cricket}} To [[throw]] a [[ball]] back to the [[wicket-keeper]] (or a [[fielder]] at that position) from somewhere in the [[field]].
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[say]] in [[reply]]; to [[respond]].
#: {{ux|en|to '''return''' an answer;&emsp; to '''return''' thanks;&emsp; “Do it yourself!” she '''returned'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Dickens Tale of Two Cities|chapter=A Hand at Cards|page=205|passage=“No!” '''returned''' the spy. “I throw up. I confess that we were so unpopular with the outrageous mob, that I only got away from England at the risk of being ducked to death {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:James Maisie|chapter=XII|page=132|passage=“Ah my good friend, I do look out,” the young man '''returned''' while Maisie helped herself afresh to bread and butter.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|computing}} To [[relinquish]] control to the [[call]]ing [[procedure]].
# {{lb|en|transitive|computing}} To pass (data) back to the calling procedure.
#: {{ux|en|This function '''returns''' the number of files in the directory.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|dated}} To [[retort]]; to throw back.
#: {{ux|en|to '''return''' the lie}}
#* {{RQ:Dryden Absalom|chapter=Reader|page=2|passage=And novv, if you are a Malitious ''Reader'', I expect you ſhould '''return''' upon me, that I affect to be thought more Impartial than I am.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[report]], or bring back and make known.
#: {{ux|en|to '''return''' the result of an election}}
#* {{RQ:King James Version|Exodus|19|8|page=156|column=2|passage=And all the people anſwered together, [...] and Moſes '''returned''' the wordes of the people vnto the {{smc|Lord}}.}}
# {{lb|en|Commonwealth|by extension|dated in the US}} To [[elect]] to a certain office.
#: {{ux|en|he was '''returned''' as a Member of Parliament}}
# {{lb|en|fencing}} To give a [[thrust]] or [[cut]] after [[parry]]ing a sword-thrust.

